--> How to Run It

Open PowerShell as Administrator

Navigate to the folder containing the script, e.g.:

cd C:\Scripts


Run the script:

.\MakeAllReposPrivate.ps1

(or)

.\MakeAllReposPublic.ps1


Watch it convert each repo — progress will be displayed and also logged at
📄 C:\RepoChangeLog.txt


If you see an error like “git is not recognized…”, then:

Install Git for Windows → https://git-scm.com/download/win

During setup, choose “Add Git to PATH”

Close PowerShell → reopen → retry the script

2️You need “repo” scope authorization

When you logged in via gh auth login, you may not have granted full repo scope.
Fix:

gh auth logout
gh auth login


Then choose:

 GitHub.com

 HTTPS

 Authenticate Git with your GitHub credentials

 Login with a web browser

When your browser opens, grant “repo” access (this allows editing repo visibility).

Then confirm:

gh auth status


It should say:

✓ Logged in to github.com as PMSQLDBA (repo, gist)

3️Re-run one repo manually to test

Try this manually to confirm permissions:

gh repo edit PMSQLDBA/SQL-Server-DB-Security --visibility private --confirm


If that works (you see no error), the script will work for all.

4️Check rate limits (optional)

If you’re changing a lot of repos quickly, GitHub might throttle. You can check:

gh api rate_limit

 Next Step (Recommended)

Once you’ve installed Git and re-authenticated with repo scope:

Re-run your script:

.\MakeAllReposPrivate.ps1

You should now see most lines turning into:

 PMSQLDBA/SQL-Server-DB-Security changed to private


